                <!-- end section -->
                <section id="lineSecurity" class="section">
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col-md-12 left-align">
                            <h2 class="dark-text">Web Security <hr></h2>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>
                    <!-- end row -->
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <p>
                                Use the <srmenu>Configuration->Configure Web Security...</srmenu> menu of the <strong>Server Manager</strong> to configure the security (stored in the <i>Security.xml</i> file).
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                The <strong>Web Security</strong> defines the login process to the <b>Web Report Server</b> through <strong>Security Provider</strong> and all the user rights through the <strong>Security Groups</strong>.
                            </p>
                            <!-- end col -->
                        </div>
                        <!-- end row -->
                    </div>
                    <div id="lineSecurity_provider" class="row" style="padding-top:35px">
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <h4>Security Provider</h4>
                            <p>
                                The <strong>Security Provider</strong> defines how the authentication is done when a user wants to login.<br />
                                All the default <strong>Security Providers</strong> are defined in *.cshtml files in the <i>/Security/Providers</i> Repository sub-folder.<br />


                                A <code>Security Script</code> is executed to define the name and the groups of the user. If the user has no group, the authentication has failed.<br />

                                Depending on his type, a security provider may have parameters (<eg>e.g. to configure the LDAP Server name for the LDAP Authentication Provider</eg>).
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-info">
                                The following <strong>Security Providers</strong> are available and can be easily modified.<br />
                                Check the <code>Security Script</code> to have more information.
                                <ul>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Basic Authentication</b> shows a simple user/password authentication from the user name and password of the login window.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Basic Windows Authentication</b> use Windows authentication to validate the user name and password, and define the groups: Windows group names must match group names defined in the security.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Database Authentication</b> shows a sample of querying the database to authenticate and get the groups.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Integrated Windows Authentication</b> is the same as Basic Windows Authentication but relies on the integrated authentication provided by IIS.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>LDAP Authentication</b> shows a sample of using the LdapConnection object to authenticate and get the groups.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>JWT</b> shows how to authenticate a user having a JSON Web Token (JWT) generated by another server sharing a secret key.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>OpenID</b> shows the use of the OpenID authentication.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>No Security</b> just adds a security group to the user.
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>

                            <p>
                                Three optional features can be enabled in the security configuration (they are disabled by default):
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <strong>Two-Factor Authentication</strong> (<code>Enable Two-Factor Authentication</code>): requires two scripts, one <code>Generation Script</code> to generate the code sent by Email or SMS, one <code>Check Script</code> to validate the code.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <strong>Reset Password</strong> (<code>Enable Reset Password</code>): requires two scripts, one to initiate the reset (e.g. send an email with a reset link), one to apply the new password.
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <strong>Change Password</strong> (<code>Enable Change Password</code>): allows users having a login defined in the security file to change their password. An optional <code>Change Password Script</code> may be defined to customize the behavior.
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>
                                Set the option <code>Hide login user name and password</code> to true to hide the user name and password fields in the login page (<eg>e.g. for the Integrated Windows Authentication</eg>). This option replaces the <i>HideLoginPassword</i> property of the <i>appsettings.json</i> file, which is still supported for compatibility.
                            </p>

                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-demo">
                                Script sample for the Basic Authentication Provider:
                                <pre><code class="language-csharp">
@@{
    SecurityUser user = Model;
	//Basic authentication script: use the user name and password to authenticate the user and set his name and security groups...
	//user.WebUserName -> user name from the login screen
	//user.WebPassword -> password from the login screen

	user.Name = user.WebUserName; //Display name

	if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(user.WebUserName)) {
		user.AddDefaultSecurityGroup();
	}
	else if (user.WebUserName == "userName" && user.WebPassword == "password")
    {
        user.AddSecurityGroup("aGroupName");
    }
    else {
        //Authenticate and set the groups defined in the security login
        if (!user.LoginAuthentication(user.WebUserName, user.WebPassword)) {
            throw new Exception("Invalid user name or password");
        }
    }
}                            </code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <p>
                                Besides the display name and the security groups, the script may set the personal folder name (<code>user.PersonalFolderName</code>) and the business context of the user in <code>user.Properties</code>, a dictionary of key/value strings (<eg>e.g. customer, subscription tier, quotas</eg>).
                                The other scripts read it with <code>user.GetProperty("key")</code> (<eg>e.g. the Pre SQL of a data source through <code>Report.SecurityContext</code>, report or AI tool scripts</eg>). The dictionary is cleared at each login and at logout, and it is never sent to the browser.<br />
                                A script may also add to <code>user.SecurityGroups</code> a <code>SecurityGroup</code> built in memory (folders and rights, personal folder, data sources): rights defined per customer or per user can then come from a database instead of the security file.
                            </p>
                            <div id="lineSecurity_provider_name_only" class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
                                <h4>Authentication from the user name only</h4>
                                The server itself executes the <strong>Security Provider</strong> for a user who is not in front of the login page: <code>user.ForSchedule</code> is then true, <code>user.WebUserName</code> contains the user name and there is no password, no HTTP request (<code>user.Request</code> and <code>user.WebPrincipal</code> are null) and no Two-Factor Authentication.
                                This is the case for:
                                <ul>
                                    <li>every request of the <b>MCP server</b> of the AI Agents, with a personal access token or an OAuth access token: the user is loaded again at each request, so a change of his groups, or a user removed from the directory, applies immediately,</li>
                                    <li>the <b>Web Schedules</b> executed by the Seal Report Scheduler,</li>
                                    <li>the scheduled outputs having a <code>User name</code> if <code>Execute the provider for scheduled outputs</code> is set.</li>
                                </ul>
                                The script must then load the user and his groups from his name, without checking a password: a script checking the password fails and these features are refused (<eg>e.g. MCP requests answered by 401 'not authenticated by the Security Provider', web schedule disabled</eg>).
                                The default providers have a branch <code>if (user.ForSchedule)</code>:
                                <ul>
                                    <li><b>Basic Authentication</b>: <code>user.LoginAuthentication()</code> does not check the password if <code>user.ForSchedule</code> is true.</li>
                                    <li><b>Basic Windows Authentication</b> and <b>Integrated Windows Authentication</b>: the user and his groups are read from the Active Directory (<code>Groups matching: Method used to get the Windows groups</code>, <i>Domain</i> if empty), the account of the server must be allowed to read the directory.</li>
                                    <li><b>LDAP Authentication</b>: the user is searched with the <i>Service account</i> parameters (user name and password, or the account of the server if empty, search base and user filter).</li>
                                    <li><b>Database Authentication</b>: the user is checked by the <code>Service user query</code> executed with the <code>Service connection string</code>.</li>
                                    <li><b>JWT</b>, <b>OpenID</b> and <b>Claims Principal</b>: the token or the claims are not available, the user is loaded from the logins of the security (<code>user.LoginAuthentication()</code>); change the script to load him from another source (<eg>e.g. a database</eg>).</li>
                                    <li><b>No Security</b>: the users have no name, the MCP access tokens and the web schedules are not available.</li>
                                </ul>
                                The providers are templates: the script of a security already configured is kept in the security file and must be adapted if needed.
                                For a single sign-on, the script must also set <code>user.WebUserName</code> with a stable identifier (<eg>e.g. the claim 'preferred_username' or 'email'</eg>): the MCP authorizations, the access tokens and the web schedules are bound to this name.
                            </div>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
                                <h4>Test your security provider</h4>
                                Once your <strong>Security Provider</strong> is configured, use the helper <code>Test a login</code> to fully test a login process with given name and password.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>
                    <!-- end row -->
                    <div id="lineSecurity_group" class="row" style="padding-top:35px">
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <h4>Security Group</h4>
                            <p>
                                After a successful authentication process, the user belongs to one or several <code>Security Groups</code>.<br /><br />

                                A <strong>Security Group</strong> allows to configure:<br />
                                - which repository folders are published for the user,<br />
                                - if the user can view reports,<br />
                                - if the user has personal folders,<br />
                                - if a Startup report is executed at login,<br />

                                A <strong>Security Group</strong> has a collection of repository <code>Folders</code>, each folder has a given relative path and right (from <b>No Right</b> to <b>Edit Reports</b>).
                            </p>
                            <h5>Consistency check</h5>
                            <p>
                                When the security is saved, the <b>Server Manager</b> checks each group and shows the warnings before saving (<code>SealSecurity.CheckConsistency()</code>):
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>an <b>AI Agent</b> assigned whose tools will be refused: an agent editing the Data Sources (<eg>e.g. Data Source Manager</eg>) without the right <b>Read and write</b> on the repository folder <code>\Sources</code> or without the <b>Metadata and SQL</b> level, an agent writing reports (<eg>e.g. Schedule &amp; Output</eg>) without a report folder having the right <b>Edit</b> or a personal folder for reports, a skill creating reports from SQL with the <b>Metadata only</b> level. Only the agents dedicated to the task are checked: an optional skill of an agent (<eg>e.g. 'manage-data-source' for the Report Designer</eg>) is simply refused without the right;</li>
                                <li>an agent, a data source or an output device that does not exist;</li>
                                <li>a sensitive repository folder published (<code>\Security</code>, <code>\Settings</code>, <code>\SpecialFolders</code> which contains the personal folders of all the users, or the root), or a repository folder in the Reports folder (ignored);</li>
                                <li>the provider <b>No Security</b> with the MCP server or the Seal Report Scheduler: the users have no user name, so the MCP access tokens and the web schedules are not available;</li>
                                <li>a sub-report folder that does not exist in the <code>\SubReports</code> folder, or the root of the sub-reports declared (all the sub-reports are then protected).</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>
                                The checks are done per group: a right given by another group of the user is not considered.
                                The AI tool <code>security_check</code> (skill <code>audit-security-and-activity</code>, agent <b>System Admin</b>) returns the same warnings with the effective security of the current user, to explain why an agent or a tool is refused. It checks the groups of the user, or all the groups if the user can execute the System reports.
                            </p>
                            <h5 id="lineSecurity_subreports">Sub-report folders</h5>
                            <p>
                                The sub-reports of the repository folder <code>\SubReports</code> are not published in the folders: they are executed from the navigation links of the reports (<eg>e.g. Customer->Customer Detail</eg>).
                                The server only accepts a navigation (sub-report, drill or report execution) that has been issued in the result of the report: the sub-report and the values of its restrictions come from a cell displayed to the user, a navigation built or changed in the browser is refused.
                                The sub-report must be located in the repository, a report reached by a navigation in the <code>\Reports</code> folder requires a right on its folder, and a report of a personal folder must be in the personal folder of the user.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                By default, a sub-report can be executed by all the users from the reports they can execute. The <code>Sub-report folders</code> of a <strong>Security Group</strong> restrict the sub-reports of a folder (path relative to <code>\SubReports</code>) to some groups:
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>a folder is <b>protected once a group declares it</b>: only the users of a group having the right <code>Execute</code> on the folder can then execute its sub-reports, the folders declared by no group stay free;</li>
                                <li><code>Include sub-folders</code> applies the definition to the sub-folders, the most specific folder declared by a group wins;</li>
                                <li><code>Execute</code> set to false protects a folder without giving the right (<eg>e.g. to exclude <code>\HR\Salary</code> from the group having the right on <code>\HR</code></eg>);</li>
                                <li>the rights of the groups of the user are added.</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>
                                The navigation links to a sub-report that the user cannot execute are not displayed in the report result.
                                <eg>Example:</eg> move the sub-report <i>Salary Detail.srex</i> to <code>\SubReports\HR</code> and add the sub-report folder <code>\HR</code> to the group <b>HR</b>: the other users do not see the link and cannot execute it.
                                The System report <i>100 Configuration - Security Summary</i> lists the sub-report folders of the groups (prefix <code>[SubReports]</code>) in the tab Folders.
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
                                <h4>Rights on the data</h4>
                                The rights on the sub-reports control who can open them, not the rows they return: when the data must be filtered per user, the filter must be done in the Data Source (<eg>e.g. a restriction or a Pre SQL using <code>Report.SecurityContext</code></eg>), not only in the report having the navigation link.
                            </div>
                            <h5>Download and Upload rights</h5>
                            <p>
                                In addition to its right, each folder has two flags, both disabled by default:
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>Allow Report Download</code>: the user can download the report files (*.srex) of the folder from the <b>Web Report Server</b>. Downloading a report is the only way to extract its definition from the web (<eg>e.g. to edit it with the Report Designer</eg>).</li>
                                <li><code>Allow Upload</code>: the user can upload files to the folder.</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>
                                The same two flags exist on the <b>Security Group</b> for the user's <b>Personal folder</b>.
                            </p>
                            <h5>Report code level</h5>
                            <p>
                                Web users and <b>AI Agents</b> can save reports (upload, AI tools). The <code>Report code level</code> of the <b>Security Group</b> defines the code they can write in a report:
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>Metadata only</code> (default): elements, restrictions, views and outputs built from the Data Source metadata, no SQL and no script.</li>
                                <li><code>Metadata and SQL</code>: also custom SQL of elements and restrictions, SQL models, enumerated lists (except the lists of the values of a column generated by the AI tools) and joins defined in the report, restriction expressions other than <code>[restriction] AND/OR/NOT</code>, and the raw SQL queries of the AI tools (<code>database_*</code>, <code>report_create_from_sql</code>). Dynamic filters set for security purpose are not applied to raw SQL.</li>
                                <li><code>Metadata, SQL and scripts</code>: also Razor scripts (cell scripts, model and task scripts, custom templates, Pre/Post SQL...), connections, tasks and table templates (with their parameters) defined in the report, and code rendered in the browser. Any text starting with <code>@@</code> is a Razor script.</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>
                                The level of a user is the highest level of his groups. It replaces the former <code>SQL models</code> option: a group saved with this option enabled is read as <b>Metadata and SQL</b>.
                                For an AI tool, the level applied is the lowest between the level of the user and the <code>Maximum report code level</code> of the <b>AI Agent</b> (<eg>e.g. set it to Metadata for an agent published to many users</eg>), the raw SQL queries of the tools included.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                Code rendered in the browser is also checked below the <b>Scripts</b> level:
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>The view parameters rendered without encoding by their view template (<eg>e.g. the colors, sizes or CSS of a chart, the additional JavaScript of the report</eg>, detected from the <code>@@Raw()</code> calls of the template and its partial templates, or declared with <code>CodeLevel</code> in the template configuration): a value must be one of the values proposed by the template, a plain value without quote, parenthesis, brace, angle bracket, equal sign, ampersand or backslash, or a value of the type expected by the parameter:
                                    <ul>
                                        <li>colors (<code>chartjs_colors</code>, <code>echarts_colors</code>, <code>plotly_colors</code>...): a list of CSS color names, <code>#hex</code>, <code>rgb()</code>, <code>rgba()</code>, <code>hsl()</code> or <code>hsla()</code> with numeric arguments (<eg>e.g. ['#1f77b4','green']</eg>);</li>
                                        <li>color mappings (<code>chartjs_color_mappings</code>, <code>plotly_color_mappings</code>): label and color pairs, the labels quoted without quote, backslash, angle bracket or new line (<eg>e.g. 'ok':'green','ko':'red'</eg>); the colors and the mappings are saved in a canonical form;</li>
                                        <li>CSS (<code>*_css</code>, <code>additional_css</code>): declarations <code>property: value;</code> without <code>url(</code>, <code>expression(</code>, <code>@@import</code>, angle bracket, brace, double quote or backslash; CSS classes (<code>*_class</code>): class names;</li>
                                        <li>HTML summaries (<code>model_summary</code>, <code>report_summary</code>, <code>restrictions_header</code>, <code>restrictions_footer</code>): no active content, as for the Guide;</li>
                                        <li><code>widget_exec_report</code>: the path of an existing report of the Reports or the Personal folder that the user can execute.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                    Any other value, and the JavaScript of <code>additional_js</code>, requires the Scripts level.
                                </li>
                                <li>The HTML texts (report <code>Guide</code>, email body): no active content (script, frame, embedded object, event handler or script URL).</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>
                                The AI tools managing the Data Sources (<code>datasource_*</code>) require the <b>Metadata and SQL</b> level, and a text starting with <code>@@</code> in their arguments (table SQL, column SQL, join clause, names...) requires <b>Metadata, SQL and scripts</b>: it would be compiled as a Razor script each time the source is used.
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-info">
                                The level is checked only when a report is written, and only for new or modified content: the SQL and scripts already saved in a report are kept when it is modified (<eg>e.g. an agent changing a column of a report having a cell script</eg>), removing content is always allowed, and existing reports can always be executed.<br />
                                An uploaded report, or a file renamed or copied as a report, is checked as a new report. A report copied or moved on the server keeps its content. Each refusal is written in the audit (type <code>ReportCodeRefused</code>).<br />
                                The Report Designer and the Server Manager are not checked, and the files uploaded to the Repository Folders (Danger Zone) are outside of this check.
                            </div>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-info">
                                If a user belongs to several groups, the following rules are applied:<br /><br />
                                For the <b>Reports</b> folders<br />
                                <ul>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Folders</b>: The highest right is used (No right, Execute reports / View files, Execute reports and outputs / View files, Edit schedules / View files, Edit reports / Manage files).
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Allow Report Download, Allow Upload</b>: Enabled if one group enables it.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Personal folder</b>: The highest right is used (No personal folder, Personal folder for files only, Personal folder for reports and files).
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Show empty parent folders</b>: True if one group has this flag set to true.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <b>Folders, Folder Detail and Menu Scripts</b> are executed sequentially sorted by group name.
                                    </li>
                                </ul>

                                For <b>Default values</b><br />
                                <ul>
                                    <li>
                                        The options (Edit Profile, Culture, Logo, Startup, Execution mode) are taken from the group having the highest <code>Weight</code>.
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                            <p>
                                A <b>Meta Column</b> may have a <code>Security tag</code> property. This text can be used to define rights for the designers.
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
                                <h4>List the current right definitions</h4>
                                The System Report <b>'100 Configuration - Security Summary'</b> generates also a full security summary.<br />
                                To display rights per user, it may be adapted to match the security provider used.<br />
                                <a class="btn btn-primary live-system">System: 100 Configuration - Security Summary</a>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>
                    <div id="lineSecurity_repo" class="row" style="padding-top:35px">
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <h4>Repository Folders</h4>
                            <p>
                                A <strong>Security Group</strong> can also publish <b>Repository Folders</b>: folders located anywhere under the repository root, outside the <b>Reports</b> tree (<eg>e.g. the /Databases folder to let users manage the local Excel files loaded by a data source</eg>).<br />
                                Each <b>Repository Folder</b> is defined with a relative path, a right (<b>No right</b>, <b>Read only</b> to view and download files, <b>Read write</b> to manage files) and an <code>Allow Upload</code> flag.
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-danger">
                                Publishing repository folders exposes files outside the Reports tree: grant these rights carefully and prefer <b>Read only</b> whenever possible.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>
                    <!-- end row -->
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col-md-12 text-center">
                            <div class="caption">
                                <b>Security Objects</b>
                            </div>
                            <img src="images/CD-Security.svg" alt="" class="img-thumbnail rounded">
                            <div>
                                <ck>Check also <a href="~/Help/Index.html" target="_blank">Seal Report Library (Seal.Model namespace)</a> for more information</ck>.
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>


                    <div id="lineSecurity_pub" class="row" style="padding-top:35px">
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <h4>Custom Publications</h4>
                            <p>
                                In addition to the security applied on report folders, dedicated scripts (properties of a <b>Security Group</b>) allow to modify the reports published to the logged user.<br />
                                <ul>
                                    <li>
                                        <code>Folder Script</code>: Optional script executed to define/modify the folders published in the <b>Web Report Server</b>.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <code>Folder Detail Script</code>: Optional script executed to define/modify the reports published in the <b>Web Report Server</b> for a given folder.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <code>Menu Script</code>: Optional script executed to define/modify the reports of the logged user.
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                If the user belongs to several groups, scripts are executed sequentially sorted by group name.
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-demo">
                                <code>Folder Detail Script</code> sample to filter 'Charts' reports in the <b>/Samples</b> folder:
                                <pre data-line="5"><code class="language-csharp">
@@{
    SecurityUser user = Model;
     if (user.FolderDetail.folder.path == @@"\Samples") {
        //Sample to filter reports
        user.FolderDetail.files = user.FolderDetail.files.Where(i => i.name.Contains("Charts")).ToList(); 
    }
}
                            </code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>

                    <div id="lineSecurity_sta" class="row" style="padding-top:35px">
                        <div class="col-md-12">
                            <h4>Startup Report</h4>
                            <p>
                                Once logged with the <strong>Web Report Server</strong>, a startup report defined in the <code>Report executed on startup</code> property of a <b>Security Group</b> can be executed automatically.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                This allows to fully customize the login page for given users.
                            </p>
                            <div class="bs-callout bs-callout-info">
                                <h4>
                                    Check the following sample Startup Reports
                                </h4>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>
                                        <strong>40-Startup Report</strong>: Defines a Welcome page with several options to execute reports and to browse specific folders.
                                    </li>
                                    <li>
                                        <strong>41-Startup Folder</strong>: Forces a specific folder after the login and disable most of navigation and execution links.
                                    </li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- end col -->
                    </div>

